C1 kinetic carrier: Int_0^R r^2 [Int gamma f dv] dr = d/dt Int (mu_K^2 h/K) r^2 dr + d/dt W(t,R) + V_F(t,R);
   the right side contains no carrier variable: True ; net transfer into the polarization charge with a free-falling boundary = 0
C2 kinetic loss S = Int gamma f dv with f = n g, Int g = 1: n/2 -> at n = 0: 0
MA1 (f = (v^2 - 1/2) e^{-v^2}, sign-indefinite): density 0 , loss sqrt(pi)/2 -> bites
C3 collisionless fluid limit: Jeans root -K(n_d + rho) at k -> 0, residual: 0
C3 growing root k^2 coefficient c1 = (K*n_d**2 - lambda_s*mu_K**2*n_d - lambda_s*mu_K**2*rhobar + mu_K**2*rhobar*sigma**2)/(mu_K**2*(n_d + rhobar))
   c1 - [sigma^2 rho/(n_d + rho) - lambda_s + K n_d^2/(mu_K^2 (n_d + rho))], residual: 0
C3 large k: (sum of roots)/k^2 -> alpha*sigma**2 + sigma**2 ;  (product)/k^2 -> -lambda_s*(alpha + 1)*(K*rhobar + mu_K**2*sigma**2) ;  small root -> product/sum -> -lambda_s*(K*rhobar + mu_K**2*sigma**2)/sigma**2
   small root + lambda_s (K rho + mu_K^2 sigma^2)/sigma^2, residual: 0
   so the growing branch saturates at omega^2 = -lambda_s (K rho + mu_K^2 sigma^2)/sigma^2: bounded for sigma > 0, rate ~ sqrt(lambda_s)/sigma x Jeans;
   it is no faster than Jeans iff lambda_s (K rho + mu_K^2 sigma^2)/sigma^2 <= K (n_d + rho), i.e. sigma^2 >= lambda_s up to O(n_d/rho, mu_K^2 sigma^2/(K rho)).
C3 numbers: lambda_s = 188 (km/s)^2 at ell = 1 kpc (alpha = 1); with sigma at the forest line (9.199 km/s) the band is removed only if ell <= 0.67 kpc (alpha = 1; ell_max ~ 1/sqrt(alpha)). A CDM-cold carrier (sigma -> 0) has the band with no cutoff in this limit. At ell = 1 kpc and sigma = 9.199 km/s the saturated rate is 1.49 x Jeans.
runtime 0.5 s
